 #:seon.task{:name "one-agents-current-state-costs-hundreds-of-megabytes"
  :plan "`seon.context/current-state` for one agent costs about 380 ms and 433 MB on default (lane dc-lifecycle, 2026-10-10, HEAD a6cbe4fcf); it runs once per model call to build the block. What the work must do: read the agent's one-hop attachments (task, its references, latest evaluations, proofs) and render them, proportional to what the block shows, not to the program. Profile it with `seon.profile/measure`, name the armed functions that allocate, fix the owner; done when one agent's block is under 50 ms and 50 MB on default. Read `docs/seon/plan.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "reached-reds-are-invisible-to-the-agent"
  :plan "When a task's proofs are green but `:seon.agent/reached-red` names tests its change broke, `seon.context/task-done?` is false, yet neither `my.task/current` nor `seon.context/current-state` shows those tests, so the agent cannot see why it is not done. Observed on default 2319152dc (chain experiment 1, attempt b83a488f2b98): its proof went green at call 3, `seon.sci.forgive-test/the-reader-reads-a-reply-by-its-line-rules` (8 failures) and `seon.sci.forgive-test/recorded-replies-read-into-the-same-segments` (3) were reached-red, and calls 4-15 repeated (my.task/current) and 'the task is :done' until :capped; a send-back to Design+Prove was the right move and never considered. Correct: `my.task/current` and the block's task section answer the reached reds beside the proofs, each with its failing data, as `seon.context/proofs` does for a red proof. Read `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/chain-experiment-1.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-step-proof-cannot-observe-its-agents-test-evidence"
  :plan "A step proof that reads test evidence through the program cannot be satisfied: a test member runs on its own branch, and `seon.test.runner/latest-results` reads only runs recorded for the database's own branch, so `seon.context/proofs` inside the member answers :pending for every proof the agent's branch ran red. Observed on default 2319152dc (chain experiment 1, attempt 08d6fe652434): the step proof `(every? #{:red} (map :seon.context/proof-status (:seon.context/proof-list (seon.context/proofs (seon.db/db) 'seon.sci.forgive/trailing-prose-implement))))` stayed red while the same read at the agent's REPL answered [:red]; the agent spent 15 calls and ended :capped. TASK-CHAIN §3b says a step proof runs against the subject's revision and the branch program supplied as explicit test inputs; decide the input (the held step's branch and commit) and supply it to the member, so Prove's proof can assert observed red on the agent's program. Read `seon.test/run`, `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/chain-experiment-1.md` and `docs/seon/schedule/cards/TASK-CHAIN.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "the-current-state-shows-the-branch-diff-since-its-merge-base"
             :plan "(hybrid-done-and-merge M14) The branch is the proposal. `seon.context/current-state` carries a derived `:seon.context/changes` (new key in `resources/seon/schemas/seon.context.edn`, never stored): the entities the branch added, changed and removed between its `:seon.task/merge-base` and its head, from `seon.cluster.source/changed-identities` (the hybrid's `seon.program/changed-identities` is the same operation; it is `seon.program/three-way`'s sibling) over program and cluster attributes, grouped by family (functions, tests, schemas, namespaces, task facts, other cluster facts), each entry with the form that digs into it (`my.program/diff`, a pull); `my.program/diff` reads the same base. Red proof: `seon.context-test/the-current-state-shows-the-branch-diff-since-its-merge-base` (after one defn, one deftest and one `my.task/update!` the block holds `:seon.context/changes` with one entry each under :functions, :tests, :task-facts, each with a readable `:seon.render/form`; an unchanged branch holds no `:seon.context/changes`). Cost: at most 5 ms per eval request on a ten-change branch (0.8 ms warm for the diff). After M3. Read `seon.profile/measure` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/hybrid-done-and-merge.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-held-steps-instructions-are-shown-whole-in-its-context"
             :plan "The agent's own task text reaches it cut. Observed (chain experiment 3, H1, default 98720b4eb): a step's 2,473-character text showed 1,024 characters and `…+1407` in `seon.context/current-state` and `my.task/current`; attempt 1 spent calls 10-15 recovering it with `subs` and ended :capped. The text a step carries is now its `:seon.task/plan`. The cut is the value printer's string bound `:seon.render.profile/max-string-length` (`seon.render.value/cut-node`) applied to the block's task section like any string, although AGENTS.md says an agent's own values are never clipped. Correct, at the producer: `seon.context/current-state` renders the held task's `:seon.task/plan` as the block's own data whole, outside the string bound (`seon.context/task-sections` already pulls it), so the value printer takes no schema-specific branch; the printer's one budget is spread over the rest of the block's shape. Proof: `seon.context-test/a-held-steps-instructions-are-shown-whole-in-its-context` (a step with a 3,000-character intent: the printed block holds all 3,000 and no `:seon.render.value/elision` for that key; red on HEAD), and the printed block stays within its token budget. Read `seon.render.value/render-ai` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/chain-experiment-3.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-chain-step-merged-before-costs-its-diff-once"
  :plan "An Implement step's block costs about 80 ms and 165 MB more than its Prove step's (measured 2026-10-13 in `seon.context-test/a-steps-block-names-its-chain`: 91 ms, 171.7 MB against 8 ms, 6.1 MB): `seon.context/chain` calls `seon.cluster.source/merge-accepted` for each merged step before it at every block read, though a merge commit's moved declarations never change. Profile `merge-accepted` on that fixture, make it do only the one commit's diff, and hold its answer in `seon.cache` keyed by the merge commit, proven by a `seon.profile/measure` assertion on the Implement block read."}

 #:seon.task{:name "every-step-shows-the-file-findings-its-merge-would-refuse"
             :plan "Done means the merge would accept, for every step (root ruling after Run 22, `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/run-22.md#Frictions` F64). seon.context/unmet (deleted) names the functions its merge refuses for a missing contract (`:uncontracted`) for Prove and Implement alike, but not the merge's two file checks: `seon.cluster.source/prepare-merge!`'s file proof (`:seon.source/write-back-error` `:not-analyzable`, a declaration naming a namespace its file does not require) and `seon.cluster.source/lint-errors` (`:seon.source/lint-error`). Today the agent learns them only from the refused merge's reason on its next attempt (`seon.cluster.agent/failed-pass!`). `seon.context/step-observation` should observe the same file proof and lint on the branch before done, so `seon.context/step-status` keeps the step :working and `seon.context/step-section` names each finding (file, row, rule, message). It needs the checkout (`seon.merge/checkout`) and the candidate texts that file proof yields without a test run; measure its cost per verdict with `seon.profile/measure` before choosing when it runs."}
