 #:seon.task{:name "current-state-after-a-send-back-costs-the-block-before-it"
             :plan "After `my.task/send-back!` (outside-1, `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-14/outside-1.md`, calls 17-18) `(seon.context/current-state)` answered in 3,580 ms and 3,076 ms, 6.1 GB allocated, 2.9 s of it in its evaluate stage; call 1 on the same task, before any send-back, was 324 ms evaluate, 2,347 ms request and 300.8 MB. Cause not isolated: the runaway proof thread 241 of that run (a timed-out test body that kept its branch) was still live, and calls 2-13 each paid a 2.9-3.7 s, 5-8 GB test step until it exited. Reproduce first: a chain on a disposable branch, a send-back, then two `current-state` reads with `:seon.repl/ms` and `:seon.repl/alloc-mb`. Done when the block read after a send-back costs what a block read before it does (a `seon.profile/measure` assertion on parent and change in one run), or the cost is named to its producing function. Read `seon.context/current-state`."}
